/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - For a simple compressible substance any property z can be written as z = z(x, y), where x and y are two independent properties; for example p = p(T, v).
   - dz = (∂z/∂x)ydx + (∂z/∂y)xdy = M dx + N dy
   - The subscript names the property held fixed.
   - A partial derivative is a slope along a line on the property surface: (∂p/∂T)v is the slope of a constant-volume line on a p–T diagram, and (∂p/∂v)T is the slope of an isotherm on a p–v diagram.
   - M dx + N dy is exact when it is the differential of some function z.
   - If the test holds, the integral of dz between two states is the same on every path and the integral round any cycle is zero: z is a property (a point function).
   - Properties such as p, v, T, u, h and s have exact differentials.
   - Take three properties x, y and z linked by one equation of state, so each is a function of the other two.
   - Reciprocity: a slope and its inverse, taken with the same property held fixed.
   - (∂x/∂y)z(∂y/∂z)x(∂z/∂x)y = −1
*/
