/* book Worked example 2: Q: 1 kg of air (ideal gas, cv = 0.718 kJ/(kg·K), R = 0.287 kJ/(kg·K)) goes reversibly from state 1 (300 K, 0.861 m³/kg) to state 2 (600 K, 1.722 m³/kg). Path A: heat at constant volume to 600 K, then expand isothermally at 600 K. Path B: expand isothermally at 300 K, then heat at constant volume to 600 K. Find the heat and the entropy change on each path, and say which of δq and ds is exact.
   1. Constant-volume heating from 300 K to 600 K: q = cvΔT = 0.718 × 300 = 215.4 kJ/kg and Δs = cvln 2 = 0.718 × 0.6931 = 0.4977 kJ/(kg·K).
   2. Isothermal doubling of volume at temperature T: q = RT·ln 2 and Δs = R·ln 2 = 0.287 × 0.6931 = 0.1989 kJ/(kg·K).
   3. Path A: q = 215.4 + 0.287 × 600 × 0.6931 = 215.4 + 119.4 = 334.8 kJ/kg; Δs = 0.4977 + 0.1989 = 0.6966 kJ/(kg·K).
   4. Path B: q = 0.287 × 300 × 0.6931 + 215.4 = 59.7 + 215.4 = 275.1 kJ/kg; Δs = 0.1989 + 0.4977 = 0.6966 kJ/(kg·K).
   5. Test of δq = cvdT + (RT/v)dv: ∂(cv)/∂v = 0 but ∂(RT/v)/∂T = R/v, so δq is not exact. Test of ds = (cv/T)dT + (R/v)dv: both cross-derivatives are 0, so ds is exact.
   Answer: The heat differs, 334.8 kJ/kg on path A and 275.1 kJ/kg on path B, but the entropy change is 0.6966 kJ/(kg·K) on both. Heat is a path function; entropy is a property. */
