/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - The natural variables of U are entropy and volume, and no laboratory controls entropy.
   - Helmholtz function: A = U − TS, also written F and called the Helmholtz free energy.
   - U is internal energy, H enthalpy (kJ); T is absolute temperature (K); S is entropy (kJ/K).
   - Start from dU = T dS − p dV for a closed system that does only pV work.
   - In both new differentials the coefficient of dT is −S: (∂G/∂T)p = −S, so G falls as T rises, faster for the phase with more entropy.
   - So −ΔA is the largest total work at constant T, and −ΔG is the largest non-pV (electrical or chemical) work at constant T and p.
   - For a pure substance the Gibbs function per mole is the chemical potential.
*/
