/* book Worked example 2: Q: Water freezes at 273.15 K and 1 atm with latent heat 333.5 kJ/kg. Taking Δh and Δs of freezing as constant over ±10 K, find Δg for freezing 1 kg at 273.15 K, at 263.15 K and at 283.15 K, and say what each result means. Also find Δa at 273.15 K, with v = 0.0010002 m³/kg for water and 0.0010908 m³/kg for ice.
   1. Freezing releases heat: Δh = −333.5 kJ/kg. At the normal freezing point, Δs = Δh/T = −333.5/273.15 = −1.2209 kJ/(kg·K).
   2. At 273.15 K: Δg = Δh − TΔs = −333.5 + 273.15 × 1.2209 = −333.5 + 333.5 = 0. Ice and water are in equilibrium.
   3. At 263.15 K (supercooled water): Δg = −333.5 + 263.15 × 1.2209 = −333.5 + 321.3 = −12.2 kJ/kg. Negative, so freezing goes on its own.
   4. At 283.15 K: Δg = −333.5 + 283.15 × 1.2209 = −333.5 + 345.7 = +12.2 kJ/kg. Positive, so water cannot freeze; ice melts instead.
   5. Δa at 273.15 K: at constant T and p, Δa = Δg − pΔv. Δv = 0.0010908 − 0.0010002 = 9.06 × 10⁻⁵ m³/kg (ice takes more room), so Δa = 0 − 101.325 × 9.06 × 10⁻⁵ = −0.0092 kJ/kg.
   Answer: Δg = 0 at 273.15 K (equilibrium), −12.2 kJ/kg at 263.15 K (water freezes) and +12.2 kJ/kg at 283.15 K (ice melts). Δa at the freezing point is −0.0092 kJ/kg: small, but not zero. */
